{
  "title": "The Pisces star label is detached and month glosses have no direct label link in the twelve zodiac centres",
  "statement": "We traced the two requested inner rings from their indicated west starts on the pinned native Yale scans. On f70v2, the text begins at the west seam near the 09:00 ZL start, follows the narrow circular band clockwise, and returns to the west below the gap. The word right of the upper star lies inward of the upper ring with a visible parchment gap. ZL comments Central star immediately before recording this as f70v2.33, a separate Lz locus after f70v2.32 (Cc). ZL and RF read otolal and IT reads otylal. The upper fish falls between that star label and the dark mars gloss. Their centres are about 450 native pixels apart. On f72v1, the pale writing below the scales follows the circular baseline from the 09:30 west seam back to the other side of the seam. ZL f72v1.33 is a Cc locus, with no later Lz on the folio. The dark octobre gloss under the scales is separate from that peripheral text. A second independent reader confirmed the two visual classifications. We measured approximate gloss-to-label geometry on all twelve zodiac panels, excluding every word on a running circular baseline. The rounded nearest-label distances range from about 170 to 450 native scan pixels, which are not physical distances and are not comparable across scans without scale. Eleven panels put the nearest plausible discrete Voynich labels outside the inner disk. The exception is the detached Pisces star label. Close radial alignment is possible without proximity or shared enclosure, so there is no basis here for confirming a bilingual month word. The readings concern layout; no dating or ink attribution is made.",
  "program": "L4",
  "check": "Open the cited canvas regions at native scale. Independently mark the ruled inner disk, month gloss and nearest discrete Voynich word-group before consulting ZL. Track the two claimed centre strings relative to their own baselines and to the figures, verify the two locus classifications in ZL, IT and RF, and assess whether any approach to a month gloss carries a drawn link or an explicit bilingual correspondence.",
  "on_path": true,
